  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>

  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Cat care FAQ</p>
      <h1 id="page-title">Everything people ask about cats.</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Quick answers to common cat questions, from sleep habits to purring. Open any question to
        reveal the answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-shell" aria-label="Frequently asked questions about cats">
      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-1">
            Why do cats sleep so much?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-1">
          <p>
            Cats conserve energy for bursts of hunting-style activity, so long naps are normal.
            Many healthy adult cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours a day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-2">
            What does purring usually mean?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-2">
          <p>
            Purring often signals comfort and relaxation, but cats may also purr when stressed or
            recovering. Context matters, so body language is the best clue.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-3">
            Why do cats knead blankets or laps?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-3">
          <p>
            Kneading is a comforting behavior that starts in kittenhood. Adult cats often keep
            doing it when they feel safe, warm, and content.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-4">
            Should indoor cats still have scratching posts?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-4">
          <p>
            Yes. Scratching helps cats stretch, mark territory, and maintain healthy claws.
            Offering sturdy posts or pads can also protect furniture.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-5">
            Why do cats sometimes chatter at birds?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-5">
          <p>
            Chattering is thought to be linked to excitement and hunting instinct. Cats may be
            expressing frustration, anticipation, or mimicry when they spot prey they cannot reach.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2 class="faq-question">
          <button class="faq-trigger" type="button" aria-controls="answer-6">
            How can you tell when a cat wants space?
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details" id="answer-6">
          <p>
            Watch for flattened ears, a twitching tail, crouching, or walking away. Respecting
            those signals helps cats feel safe and builds trust over time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    const triggers = document.querySelectorAll('.faq-trigger');

    triggers.forEach((button) => {
      const panelId = button.getAttribute('aria-controls');
      const panel = panelId ? document.getElementById(panelId) : null;

      if (!panel) {
        return;
      }

      button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
      panel.hidden = true;

      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const isExpanded = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
        button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!isExpanded));
        panel.hidden = isExpanded;
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
